===Etymology 1===
{{inh+|en|enm|hole}}, {{m|enm|hol}}, from {{inh|en|ang|hol||orifice, hollow place, cavity}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*hol}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*hulą||hollow space, cavity}}, noun derivative of {{der|en|gem-pro|*hulaz||hollow}}, which is of uncertain ultimate origin. Related to {{m|en|hollow}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A hollow place or cavity; an excavation; a pit; a dent; a depression; a fissure.
#: {{ux|en|I made a blind '''hole''' in the wall for a peg.&emsp; {{nowrap|I dug a '''hole''' and planted a tree in it.}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Antony and Cleopatra|II|vii|passage=To be called into a huge sphere, and not to be seen to move in't, are the '''holes''' where eyes should be, which pitifully disaster the cheeks.}}
#* {{RQ:Jefferies Amateur Poacher|chapter=II|passage=Orion hit a rabbit once; but though sore wounded it got to the bury, and, struggling in, the arrow caught the side of the '''hole''' and was drawn out. Indeed, a nail filed sharp is not of much avail as an arrowhead; you must have it barbed, and that was a little beyond our skill.}}
# An opening that goes all the way through a solid body, a fabric, etc.; a perforation; a rent.
#: {{ux|en|There’s a '''hole''' in my shoe.&emsp; {{nowrap|Her stocking has a '''hole''' in it.}}}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|2 Kings|12|9|passage=The priest took a chest, and bored a '''hole''' in the lid.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1840|author={{w|Alfred, Lord Tennyson|Alfred Tennyson}}|title={{w|Godiva (poem)|Godiva}}
|passage={{...}}her palfrey’s footfall shot <br/>Light horrors thro’ her pulses: the blind walls <br/>Were full of chinks and '''holes'''; and overhead <br/>Fantastic gables, crowding, stared:{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|heading}} ''In games.''
## {{lb|en|golf}} A subsurface standard-size hole, also called [[cup]], hitting the ball into which is the object of play. Each hole, of which there are usually eighteen as the standard on a full course, is located on a prepared surface, called the [[green]], of a particular type grass.
## {{lb|en|golf}} The part of a game in which a [[player]] attempts to hit the [[ball]] into one of the holes.
##: {{ux|en|I played 18 '''holes''' yesterday.&emsp; {{nowrap|The second '''hole''' today cost me three strokes over par.}}}}
## {{lb|en|baseball}} The rear portion of the defensive team between the [[shortstop]] and the third baseman.
##: {{ux|en|The shortstop ranged deep into the '''hole''' to make the stop.}}
## {{lb|en|chess}} A [[square]] on the [[board]], with some [[positional]] [[significance]], that a player does not, and cannot in the future, [[control]] with a [[friendly]] [[pawn]].
## {{lb|en|stud poker}} A card (also called a ''hole card'') dealt face down thus unknown to all but its holder; the status in which such a card is.
## In the game of [[fives]], part of the floor of the [[court]] between the [[step]] and the [[pepperbox]].
# {{lb|en|archaeology|slang}} An excavation pit or trench.
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A [[weakness]]; a [[flaw]] or [[ambiguity]].
#: {{ux|en|I have found a '''hole''' in your argument.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=2011|author=[[w:Fun (band)|Fun]]|title=w:We Are Young|passage=But between the drinks and subtle things / The '''holes''' in my apologies, you know / {{nowrap|I’m trying hard to take it back}}}}
# {{rfv-sense|en}} {{lb|en|informal}} A container or receptacle.
#: {{ux|en|car '''hole''';&emsp; brain '''hole'''}}
# {{lb|en|physics}} In [[semiconductor]]s, a lack of an [[electron]] in an occupied band behaving like a positively charged [[particle]].
# {{lb|en|computing}} A [[security]] [[vulnerability]] in [[software]] which can be taken advantage of by an [[exploit]].
# {{lb|en|slang|derogatory}} A person's [[mouth]].
#: {{usex|en|Just shut your '''hole'''!}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} Any [[bodily]] [[orifice]], in particular the [[anus]].
# {{lb|en|Ireland|Scotland|vulgar}} [[vagina|Vagina]].
# {{senseid|en|solitary confinement}} {{lb|en|informal|with "the"}} [[solitary confinement|Solitary confinement]], a high-security [[prison cell]] often used as punishment.
#: {{synonyms|en|box}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=02 Apr 88|journal=Gay Community News|page=9|author=Ed Mead|title=AIDS hysteria sweeps prison guards' union|text=In late December a Washington State prisoner was involved in a scuffle with a guard who was trying to take him into the '''hole'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=Ahmariah Jackson; IAtomic Seven|title=Locked Up but Not Locked Down
|passage=Disciplinary actions can range from a mere write up to serious time in the '''hole'''.}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} An undesirable place to live or visit.
#: {{ux|en|His apartment is a '''hole'''!}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1887|author=Harriet W. Daly|title=Digging, Squatting, and Pioneering Life in the Northern Territory of South Australia|page=109|passage=I have often heard people say, "One can't live upon a view," and I have heard some of the most beautiful places called "awful '''holes'''," simply because of the monotonous lives led in them.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} Difficulty, in particular, debt.
#: {{ux|en|If you find yourself in a '''hole''', stop digging.}}
# {{lb|en|graph theory}} A [[chordless]] [[cycle]] in a [[graph]].
# {{lb|en|slang|rail transport}} A [[passing loop]]; a [[siding#Etymology 3|siding]] provided for trains traveling in opposite directions on a single-track line to pass each other.
#: {{ux|en|We’re supposed to take the '''hole''' at Cronk and wait for the Limited to pass.}}
# {{lb|en|North America|historical}} A [[mountain]] [[valley]].
#: {{ux|en|Jackson Hole}}

